# =============================================================================
# Starter ~/.claude/.gitignore — laid down by /coordinator:install on git init.
#
# PRINCIPLE: Track everything that represents work (your doctrine, plugins,
# wikis, lessons). Ignore only per-machine state, credentials, and regenerable
# caches/transcripts. Edit freely — this is a starting point, not a contract.
#
# THE HAZARD THIS FILE EXISTS FOR — read before you track something new.
# The moment a SECOND machine clones this repo, "per-machine state" stops being
# tidiness and becomes correctness. Three physics facts, each invisible until
# that second machine exists:
#
#   1. GENERATED CONTENT BAKES THE AUTHORING MACHINE'S ABSOLUTE PATHS. Hook
#      commands, PATH shims, forwarders and marketplace roots are materialized
#      as full paths — a Windows drive root, a macOS home directory, whatever
#      this machine happens to be. Tracking them
#      ships one machine's filesystem to another, where those paths do not
#      exist. Whichever box pushed last wins, and the loser's next pull points
#      its tooling at a drive that isn't there.
#   2. GIT CANNOT CARRY A POSIX EXEC BIT ONTO A WINDOWS CHECKOUT. Tracked
#      executables silently decay to mode 100644 and stop being runnable. Any
#      executable surface synced across macOS+Windows decays this way with no
#      error at any point.
#   3. A MARKER FILE IS A DECISION, AND SYNCING IT MAKES IT EVERYONE'S. Consent
#      markers, "which root am I", "have I run setup" stamps — tracking one
#      means one machine's answer arrives on every peer as a pull.
#
# If a surface hits any of the three, it belongs on the machine, not in this
# repo. Per-machine state has a home already: the coordinator settings home
# ($COORDINATOR_SETTINGS_HOME, default ~/.coordinator-claude-settings). Put it
# there rather than inventing a tracked-but-ignored spot here.
#
# A FOURTH TRAP, DIFFERENT IN KIND — THE DEAD-RULE TRAP. A rule added here for an ALREADY-TRACKED path does
# NOTHING — git keeps updating what is in the index regardless. So when you add
# a rule later, untrack the path too:
#
#     git rm -r --cached <path> && git commit
#
# "It's gitignored" is never evidence a path isn't syncing; only `git ls-files
# <path>` is. Add the rule BEFORE the untracking so it's in force when the
# path leaves the index.
#
# No template placeholders: copied verbatim. Plugins that produce their own
# per-machine artifacts add nested .gitignore files in their own data dirs.
# =============================================================================

# --- Coordinator per-machine state (set by /setup + orientation on THIS machine) ---
# These answer "what is true on this machine" — a cloned ~/.claude must not carry
# them to a fresh machine.
/coordinator-setup-state.yaml
/coordinator-identity.yaml
/working-repos.yaml

# settings.json and its sidecars — machine-local, MUST NOT be tracked here.
# This is hazard 1 at its sharpest. The installer materializes every hook
# command as an absolute path and stores absolute marketplace roots, so
# whichever machine pushes last rewrites the other's entire hook chain; the
# losing machine's next pull points every hook at a drive that does not exist
# there, bricking Bash/Edit/Write — the very tools needed to repair it. Two
# machines can also disagree structurally rather than only in path spelling
# (hooks arriving via --plugin-dir on one and as explicit entries on the
# other), so there is no single correct tracked value to converge on.
# The sidecars are worse, not better: .settings-last-good.json is what the
# clobber-guard AUTO-RESTORES from, so a tracked copy gets machine-absolute
# paths actively installed into the live settings.json by the repair mechanism
# itself; the hooks hash is a digest OF machine-local content and is
# meaningless off the machine that computed it.
# Each machine keeps its own. Portable preferences belong in a surface that
# carries no absolute paths.
/settings.json
/.settings-last-good.json
/.coordinator-settings-hooks.hash

# Machine-local markers — hazard 3. Each names a decision or a resolved root
# belonging to ONE machine (which content root, whether hook generation is
# enabled here). Tracking one makes a single machine's answer arrive on every
# peer as a pull, which is how a fail-open default silently re-enabled itself
# across a fleet. Canonical home for these is the settings home's
# machine-local/ dir, not this repo.
# ANCHORED on purpose: each of these is root-only by definition (there is
# exactly one "which content root" answer per checkout), so a bare, unanchored
# rule would also swallow a same-named path a plugin legitimately tracks at
# depth. Extend this block anchored, not bare.
/.doe-root
/.coordinator-hooks-disabled
/.coordinator-hooks-enabled
/.coordinator-content-root-last-seen

# --- Credentials & secrets / machine-local auth state ---
.credentials.json
settings.local.json
stats-cache.json
mcp-needs-auth-cache.json

# --- Python bytecode ---
__pycache__/
*.pyc

# --- OS cruft (macOS Finder metadata) ---
.DS_Store

# --- Session transcripts (regenerable, massive) ---
*.jsonl
# Exception: plugin test fixtures are work product, not ephemeral transcripts.
# `**` matches at any depth, so a flat plugins/<name>/tests/fixtures/ is covered.
!plugins/**/tests/fixtures/*.jsonl

